  "recognised_text": "Imagine you're running a language model with a context window of one hundred and twenty-eight thousand tokens. With standard attention, every new query token needs to compare itself against all previous tokens. But here's the problem. What if only a small fraction of those tokens are actually relevant? That's the idea behind DeepSeek Sparse Attention, introduced in DeepSeek V3.2. Instead of performing full attention over the entire context, DeepSeek introduces a lightweight component called the Lightning Indexer. Here's how it works. First, imagine our context contains thousands of tokens, each represented by a key and value. When a new query arrives, the Lightning Indexer compares that query against the historical tokens. It uses smaller projections and multiple indexing heads to calculate relevant scores. Each indexing head computes a dot product between its query representation and the historical keys, applies a ReLU activation, and contributes a weighted score. These scores are aggregated to estimate which previous tokens are most important. Now comes the interesting part. Instead of sending every token into the expensive main attention computation, DeepSeek selects only the top two thousand and forty-eight tokens with the highest indexing scores. Notice that these tokens can come from anywhere in the previous context. They're not necessarily the most recent tokens, and they don't need to be adjacent. Next, DeepSeek's main attention mechanism operates on this selected subset, computing attention weights and aggregating information from their corresponding values. This is where the computational savings come from. With dense attention, the main attention operation grows with the full context length. With sparse attention, the main attention only needs to process the selected tokens. However, there's an important catch. The Lightning Indexer still needs to score the historical context to decide which tokens to select. So sparse attention doesn't eliminate all the cost of searching through a long sequence. It replaces expensive full attention with a cheaper indexing operation, followed by attention on a much smaller subset. The key insight? Not every token needs the same amount of computational attention. By learning which tokens matter most, DeepSeek can make long context processing more efficient, while preserving useful information. And that's the fundamental idea behind DeepSeek's sparse attention."
 },
